+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and scoring
+
+This review covers the Spring Boot accounts API, its scheduled payment dispatch, persistence, security boundary, and
+the scheme, fraud, ledger and notification integrations. It is based on the implementation, `README.md`, and the
+knowledge bundle in `okf/`. A failure mode is included where the service can produce an unsafe or materially incorrect
+outcome, including a known missing control.
+
+Scores use the conventional 1–10 scale:
+
+- **Severity (S):** 1 = negligible inconvenience; 10 = customer money loss, regulatory breach, or material cross-tenant/data-protection incident.
+- **Occurrence (O):** 1 = rare; 10 = expected/frequent under normal operation or already present by design.
+- **Detection (D):** 1 = almost certain and immediate detection; 10 = unlikely to be detected before customer or downstream impact.
+- **RPN:** `S × O × D`. Prioritise first by RPN, while treating any S=10 item as release-blocking until explicitly accepted.
+
+Scores are qualitative estimates for release planning, not observed incident rates.
+
+## FMEA
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers | Effect on dependent systems and teams | Current detection / control | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| F1 | Payment is sent without fraud screening | `PaymentService` calls `PaymentDispatcher` directly; the fraud client exists but is not wired into the payment path, despite FRD-7 | A fraudulent or sanctioned payment can be sent; legitimate payments also lack the expected review status and customer messaging | Fraud team has no `HELD_FOR_REVIEW` work item; compliance and audit evidence is incomplete; scheme and ledger receive prohibited traffic | Code review/architecture inspection; no runtime metric or gate currently proves every payment was screened | 10 | 10 | 9 | 900 | Block release until every payment is screened before scheme submission. Persist the decision, fail closed on timeout/error, map `REFER` to `HELD_FOR_REVIEW`, and add tests/metrics for zero unscreened submissions. |
+| F2 | Scheme timeout loses the local payment record although the scheme accepted it | `SchemeTimeoutException` is not caught; the transaction rolls back, while the gateway may have accepted the instruction | Customer sees an error and may retry; the payment may still leave their account, with no reliable status in this service | Finance reconciliation sees a scheme settlement with no matching local payment; support and operations cannot identify or resolve it | Gateway timeout logs/HTTP error; 07:00 reconciliation may find an unmatched settlement, after impact | 10 | 5 | 8 | 400 | Catch timeout, retain an `UNKNOWN`/reconciliation state, and query the scheme by the stable `P{id}` reference before retrying. Add an exception queue, alert and operator workflow. |
+| F3 | A payment is sent twice | An operator or retry creates a new scheme reference after an ambiguous timeout, or concurrent requests pass balance/daily-limit checks | Customer is debited twice or two payees receive funds; remediation is manual | Scheme settlement and ledger postings do not match; finance reconciliation breaks and complaints volume rises | Scheme duplicate checks only protect reuse of the same reference; daily reconciliation detects some cases, not immediately | 10 | 4 | 8 | 320 | Add an idempotency key on the create API, enforce a unique business key, serialize account payment decisions, and always reuse `P{id}` plus the original ledger reference on recovery. |
+| F4 | Ledger posting is booked but reported as failed, then incorrectly reposted | Five-second ledger timeout is ambiguous; service marks `POSTING_FAILED`, and recovery is manual. A new reference would debit twice | Customer may see a temporary failure or an incorrect balance; a careless retry can cause a duplicate debit | Core ledger can contain duplicate money movements; finance must investigate exceptions and scheme/ledger totals diverge | `POSTING_FAILED` status, warning log, and daily exceptions report; detection is delayed and depends on operations | 10 | 5 | 6 | 300 | Query ledger by `ledgerReference` before retrying; make recovery idempotent and automated with the same reference, with alerting and a reconciled final state. |
+| F5 | Scheme accepts payment but ledger posting fails | Scheme submission occurs before ledger posting; the service debits its copy and marks `POSTING_FAILED` after a ledger timeout | Payment has gone out although the customer may see failure or an inconsistent balance | Scheme settlement exists without a confirmed core-ledger debit; finance reconciliation and operations must repair it | Payment status and daily exceptions report; customer notification is still sent as `PAYMENT_SENT`, which can obscure the accounting failure | 10 | 5 | 6 | 300 | Use an explicit submitted/ledger-pending state and reconciliation workflow. Do not represent an unresolved ledger debit as a normal completed payment; make customer messaging reflect the state. |
+| F6 | Queued payment is submitted on a scheme holiday or misses its execution date | `WorkingDays` excludes weekends only; bank holidays are not configured. The job runs only Monday–Friday at 06:00 and has no explicit failure/alert path | Payment can be rejected with `DT01`, sent late, or remain queued past the promised date | Customer service and operations must explain or repair delayed payments; scheme rejects and exceptions increase | Payment status, scheme rejection, and operations' “queued past date” report; no proactive holiday/calendar or job-liveness alert | 8 | 7 | 6 | 336 | Use the scheme-maintained UK bank-holiday calendar, calculate the next valid date, and alert on job failure, backlog, and any queued payment past due. Test cut-off, DST, weekends and holidays. |
+| F7 | Balance or daily-limit checks allow overspending | The service uses a replicated balance copy; concurrent requests can both pass checks, and the copy is overwritten only at 02:00 | Payment may be accepted against money already spent or a stale balance; later balance correction is confusing and may cause declined/returned payments | Ledger becomes the source of truth for an overdraft/limit breach; finance and support reconcile customer-visible and core balances | Nightly ledger balance overwrite, customer reports, and reconciliation; no transactional account locking or real-time funds reservation | 9 | 6 | 7 | 378 | Reserve funds atomically per account (or perform an authoritative ledger pre-authorisation), add optimistic/pessimistic locking, and reconcile after every dispatch rather than relying only on the nightly refresh. |
+| F8 | Payment dispatch job does not run or a queued payment remains stuck | Scheduler disabled, process unavailable at 06:00, database/job error, or an exception aborts the loop; there is no job run/queue metric in the code | Payment remains `QUEUED` and is sent later than promised, with no clear customer explanation | Scheme volume and settlement timing shift; operations and support manually chase overdue payments | `QUEUED` past-date report is the stated control, but it is retrospective | 8 | 5 | 7 | 280 | Add durable job-run heartbeats, backlog age/size alerts, per-payment retry isolation, and an operator replay mechanism. Make dispatch idempotent so replay is safe. |
+| F9 | Customer is not told about a completed, queued, or held payment | Notification service is unavailable; notifications are best effort, caught and logged, with no retry or delivery status | Customer may believe the payment failed or was not reviewed and may submit it again | Support and complaints teams receive avoidable contacts; duplicate-payment risk increases | Warning log and customer inquiry; no durable notification outbox or delivery metric | 7 | 7 | 8 | 392 | Use an outbox with retries and deduplication, track delivery status and age, and alert on failures. Keep notifications non-blocking, but make the payment status authoritative in the API. |
+| F10 | Sensitive payment or account data is disclosed | A future access-policy or tenant-claim regression; `PaymentView` returns full payee sort code/account number to any authenticated caller who can view the payment, and token tenant claims are trusted | PII/bank details can be exposed; cross-tenant disclosure is a reportable security incident | Identity, security, privacy, complaints and tenant customers are affected; fraud risk increases | JWT signature validation, resource-level `getVisible` checks, 404 concealment, and access tests; no audit/event monitoring is evident | 10 | 3 | 8 | 240 | Minimise response fields (mask bank details), enforce tenant/resource authorization in one tested policy, add deny-by-default contract tests and audit logging/alerts for sensitive reads. |
+| F11 | Ledger balance refresh is missing, stale, or applied to the wrong account | The 02:00 ledger-to-accounts feed is an external operational dependency; the service has no shown ingestion/validation path or freshness control | App shows an incorrect balance or transaction view; closure and payment decisions can be wrong | Finance, support and closure operations act on stale customer views; reconciliation and regulatory data quality suffer | Customer reports and downstream reconciliation; no documented freshness SLA/metric in the service | 9 | 5 | 8 | 360 | Monitor feed completeness, age, tenant/account counts and value totals; reject/quarantine malformed loads; surface freshness to callers and stop risky operations when stale. |
+| F12 | Account closure is approved or processed without an authoritative zero-balance check | Business rule requires a ledger check, but the visible code exposes request/list endpoints only and does not show the approval/check execution path | Funds can be stranded in a closed account, or a valid closure cannot be completed | Operations must remediate manually; ledger, statements, complaints and regulatory records can diverge | Pending closure list and manual process; no code-level ledger check or approval endpoint is visible | 10 | 3 | 9 | 270 | Implement a separate approver action with segregation of duties, current ledger balance verification, no pending payments, atomic state transition, and an audit trail. Alert on aged pending requests. |
+| F13 | Statements or regulatory return are absent, incomplete, or wrong | Statement entities/repository exist, but no statement API or monthly generation job is present; the stated 1st/5th-working-day process is not implemented here | Customer cannot obtain a required statement or receives an incorrect one | Regulatory return can be wrong or late; finance, complaints and compliance teams lack complete evidence | Manual reconciliation or regulator/customer complaint; no generation-completeness or deadline alert | 9 | 4 | 9 | 324 | Define ownership and implement/verify monthly generation from ledger closing balances, completeness checks, immutable versioned statements, and an alert before the fifth working day. |
+| F14 | Cross-tenant or unauthorized account access is granted | Malformed/incorrect JWT claims, a regression in `AccessPolicy`, missing scope/method annotation, or an unscoped repository query | Customer/staff can see or act on another customer's or bank tenant's data; payment fraud is possible | Tenant confidentiality, support boundaries, auditability and regulatory obligations fail | Signed JWT validation, tenant checks, 401/403/404 conventions, and API tests for other tenants/roles | 10 | 2 | 7 | 140 | Add property-based/negative authorization tests for every endpoint and role/scope combination, centralize tenant predicates, audit sensitive actions, and monitor denied/allowed access anomalies. |
+| F15 | Internal notes/comments or risk scores are returned to customers | Staff-only filtering is omitted or bypassed when new endpoints/serializers are added | Customer sees fraud, hardship or dispute information intended for staff; complaints and trust damage follow | Privacy, legal, support and fraud processes are compromised; disclosure may be reportable | Documentation and current controller-level filtering conventions; no single response-policy enforcement or leakage scan | 9 | 3 | 7 | 189 | Separate staff/customer DTOs, enforce `internal` filtering in service queries, add response-contract tests containing sentinel internal text, and log/audit staff-data access. |
+| F16 | Time-dependent decisions use the wrong date/time | Some code uses the injected UK `Clock`, but `ClosureRequest` and `SupportCase` constructors call `Instant.now()` directly; deployment timezone/DST or clock skew can also affect cut-off behavior | Payments may queue/send at the wrong time; records can have incorrect audit timestamps and appear out of order | Scheme cut-off, working-day operations, complaints and audit trails become unreliable | Unit/API tests with `MutableClock` cover payment timing, but direct `now()` calls can evade those tests | 7 | 4 | 7 | 196 | Inject the clock into all domain services/factories, use one UK-time policy, add DST/cut-off boundary tests, and monitor clock skew. |
