+| F18 | Record creation or updates are lost, duplicated, or silently altered | Database transaction failure, concurrent writes, missing uniqueness/idempotency constraints, or an incomplete retry/backup strategy | Cases, notes, payment history or closure evidence may be missing; customer and staff records become unreliable | Complaints cannot be answered within eight weeks; finance/regulatory evidence and operations work queues are incomplete | Database errors and manual reconciliation; no explicit audit log or end-to-end change completeness control is shown | 9 | 3 | 8 | 216 | Add durable production database controls, unique constraints and idempotency where business keys exist, immutable audit events, backup/restore verification, and reconciliation of critical records. |
+
+## Immediate release priorities
+
+1. Wire in fail-closed fraud screening and prove it with an integration test that no scheme call occurs without an
+   `ACCEPT` decision (F1).
+2. Make scheme and ledger ambiguity recoverable and idempotent, preserving local state and stable references (F2–F5).
+3. Correct working-day/holiday scheduling and add job/backlog alerts (F6, F8).
+4. Protect the balance/limit decision against concurrency and stale data (F7).
+5. Establish the ledger feed, closure, and statement controls needed by finance and regulatory reporting (F11–F13).
+6. Add production observability: dependency health, SLOs, reconciliation, queue age, notification delivery, security
+   audit events, and alerts with named on-call ownership.
+
